"""Source-based English transcriptions; see MIT conversion report for editorial changes."""
ALGEBRA = {
'quiz1': [
(1,r"Forward elimination changes $Ax=b$ to reduced row echelon form $Rx=d$, with complete solution $x=(4,0,0)^T+c_1(2,1,0)^T+c_2(5,0,1)^T$. (a) What are the $3\times3$ reduced row echelon matrix $R$ and $d$? (b) Elimination subtracts 3 times row 1 from row 2, then 5 times row 1 from row 3. What matrix connects $R,d$ to $A,b$? Use it to find $A$ and $b$.",
 r"$R=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2&-5\\0&0&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix}$, $d=(4,0,0)^T$; $E=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\-3&1&0\\-5&0&1\end{pmatrix}$; $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2&-5\\3&-6&-15\\5&-10&-25\end{pmatrix}$, $b=(4,12,20)^T$.",
 r"The two free variables give the special solutions, so the only nonzero row of $R$ is $(1,-2,-5)$ and the particular solution gives $d=(4,0,0)^T$. The row operations give $R=EA$ and $d=Eb$. Inverting the elimination matrix gives $E^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\3&1&0\\5&0&1\end{pmatrix}$. Thus $A=E^{-1}R$ and $b=E^{-1}d$, producing the stated matrices.",[1],[1,2]),
(2,r"Let $A=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&2&2\\0&3&8&7\\0&0&4&2\end{pmatrix}$. (a) Find all special solutions of $Ax=0$ and describe the whole nullspace in words. (b) Describe its column space; 'all combinations of the four columns' is not sufficient. (c) Find the reduced row echelon form of the $6\times8$ block matrix $B=\begin{pmatrix}A&A\\A&A\end{pmatrix}$.",
 r"(a) $N(A)=\operatorname{span}\{(1,0,0,0)^T,(0,-1,-1/2,1)^T\}$, a plane in $\mathbb R^4$. (b) $C(A)=\operatorname{span}\{(1,3,0)^T,(2,8,4)^T\}$, a plane in $\mathbb R^3$. (c) $\operatorname{rref}(B)=\begin{pmatrix}R&R\\0&0\end{pmatrix}$, where $R=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0&1\\0&0&1&1/2\\0&0&0&0\end{pmatrix}$.",
 r"Row reduction gives the displayed $R$, whose pivot columns are 2 and 3 and free variables are $x_1,x_4$. Setting each free variable to one in turn gives the two special solutions. The original pivot columns form a basis for the column space. For the block matrix, subtracting its top three rows from its bottom three rows gives $\begin{pmatrix}A&A\\0&0\end{pmatrix}$; reducing the top block gives the stated result.",[2],[2,3]),
(3,r"Suppose a $3\times5$ matrix $A$ has rank 3. (a) Complete the sentence: $Ax=b$ (always / sometimes but not always) has (a unique solution / many solutions / no solution). (b) What is the column space of $A$? Describe its nullspace.",
 r"(a) Always has many solutions, for every $b\in\mathbb R^3$. (b) $C(A)=\mathbb R^3$; $N(A)$ is a two-dimensional subspace of $\mathbb R^5$.",
 r"The column space has dimension 3 inside $\mathbb R^3$, so it is the whole space and every right-hand side is attainable. Rank-nullity gives nullity $5-3=2$. Adding the resulting nontrivial nullspace to any particular solution gives infinitely many solutions.",[3],[3]),
(4,r"Let $A=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\6&5\\2&4\end{pmatrix}$. (a) Explain how knowing the solutions of $Ax=b$ determines whether $b$ lies in the column space of $A$. (b) Is $b=(8,28,14)^T$ in that column space?",
 r"(a) $b\in C(A)$ if and only if $Ax=b$ is solvable. (b) Yes; $x=(3,2)^T$.",
 r"The vectors $Ax$ are exactly linear combinations of the columns, proving (a). For (b), elimination on the augmented matrix gives rows $(2,1,8)$, $(0,2,4)$, and then a zero row. Back substitution gives $x_2=2$ and $x_1=3$, so $Ax=b$.",[4],[4]),
],
'quiz2': [
(1,r"(a) Find the matrix $P$ projecting every $b\in\mathbb R^3$ onto the line in direction $a=(2,1,3)^T$. (b) Describe $C(P)$ and $N(P)$ geometrically and give a basis for each. (c) Find all eigenvectors and corresponding eigenvalues using projection geometry. What is the sum of the diagonal entries of $P$?",
 r"$P=\frac1{14}\begin{pmatrix}4&2&6\\2&1&3\\6&3&9\end{pmatrix}$. $C(P)=\operatorname{span}\{(2,1,3)^T\}$; $N(P)=\{(x,y,z):2x+y+3z=0\}=\operatorname{span}\{(3,0,-2)^T,(-1,2,0)^T\}$. Nonzero vectors in these spaces have eigenvalues 1 and 0 respectively, of multiplicities 1 and 2. The trace is 1.",
 r"The projection formula is $P=aa^T/(a^Ta)$ and $a^Ta=14$. Its range is the line spanned by $a$, and its kernel is the perpendicular plane. The two listed kernel vectors are independent and orthogonal to $a$. A projection fixes every vector on the line and sends every vector in the perpendicular plane to zero; this gives all eigenvectors, with the zero vector excluded. The diagonal sum, equal to the sum of eigenvalues, is 1.",[1,2],[1,2,3]),
(2,r"(a) Let $p=A\hat x$ be the vector in $C(A)$ nearest to $b$. If $A$ has independent columns, what equation determines $\hat x$? What vectors are perpendicular to the error $e=b-A\hat x$? What goes wrong if the columns are dependent? (b) If $A=QR$, where $Q$ has orthonormal columns and $R$ is upper triangular and invertible, find $\hat x$ and $p$ in terms of $Q,R,b$. (c) If $q_1,q_2$ are orthonormal in $\mathbb R^5$, express the projection of $b$ onto their span as their linear combination.",
 r"(a) $A^TA\hat x=A^Tb$, so $\hat x=(A^TA)^{-1}A^Tb$. Every vector in $C(A)$ is perpendicular to $e$. With dependent columns the coefficients need not be unique, although the projection $p$ remains unique. (b) $\hat x=R^{-1}Q^Tb$, $p=QQ^Tb$. (c) $p=(q_1^Tb)q_1+(q_2^Tb)q_2$.",
 r"The least-squares error satisfies $A^Te=0$, giving the normal equations. Independent columns make $A^TA$ invertible. Dependent columns make it singular and allow different coefficient vectors representing the same unique projection. For a fixed nonzero error, the full perpendicular space is $e^\perp$, which can be larger than $C(A)$; the column space is the set perpendicular to every possible least-squares error. Substituting $A=QR$ and $Q^TQ=I$ into the normal equations gives $R\hat x=Q^Tb$, hence $p=QR\hat x=QQ^Tb$. The final part follows by expanding an orthogonal projection in an orthonormal basis.",[3],[4]),
(3,r"Let $A_n$ be the $n\times n$ matrix with zero diagonal and all off-diagonal entries $-1$, i.e. $A_n=I-\mathbf1\mathbf1^T$. (a) Find its determinant; you may add all rows except the last to the last, then factor out a constant. (b) For an invertible matrix $A$, the $(1,1)$ entry of $A^{-1}$ is the ratio of what determinants? Hence find $(A_4^{-1})_{11}$. (c) Find two orthogonal eigenvectors of $A_3$ with eigenvalue 1. (d) Find its remaining eigenvalue and an eigenvector.",
 r"(a) $\det A_n=1-n$. (b) $\det M_{11}/\det A$, and $(A_4^{-1})_{11}=2/3$. (c) $(1,-1,0)^T$ and $(1,1,-2)^T$. (d) Eigenvalue $-2$ with eigenvector $(1,1,1)^T$.",
 r"The vector $\mathbf1$ has eigenvalue $1-n$, while every vector whose entries sum to zero has eigenvalue 1. Their multiplicities are 1 and $n-1$, so the determinant is $1-n$. Equivalently, the suggested row operations reduce the determinant to $1-n$ times a triangular determinant with diagonal ones. The adjugate formula gives $(A^{-1})_{11}=\det M_{11}/\det A$; for $A_4$ the minor is $A_3$, so this is $(-2)/(-3)=2/3$. The two proposed vectors in (c) have zero sum and zero dot product. The remaining eigenpair is $-2,(1,1,1)^T$.",[3,4],[5,6,7]),
],
'quiz3': [
(1,r"Suppose $u$ is a unit vector in $\mathbb R^n$ and $H=I-2uu^T$. (a) Show directly that $H^2=I$. Since $H=H^T$, what other property follows? (b) Find the eigenvalue corresponding to $u$. (c) For every nonzero $v\perp u$, show it is an eigenvector and find its eigenvalue and multiplicity. Is $H$ diagonalizable? Explain. (d) Find $H_{ii}$, the trace, and separately the sum of eigenvalues.",
 r"(a) $H^2=I$ and $H$ is orthogonal. (b) $-1$. (c) $1$ with multiplicity $n-1$; $H$ is diagonalizable. (d) $H_{ii}=1-2u_i^2$, and both sums are $n-2$.",
 r"Since $u^Tu=1$, $H^2=I-4uu^T+4u(u^Tu)u^T=I$. Thus $H^TH=I$, so $H$ is orthogonal. Also $Hu=-u$, whereas $Hv=v$ when $u^Tv=0$. An orthonormal basis of $u^\perp$ together with $u$ forms an eigenbasis. Finally, summing the diagonal gives $n-2\sum u_i^2=n-2$, agreeing with $-1+(n-1)$.",[1],[1]),
(2,r"Suppose $A$ is a positive definite symmetric $n\times n$ matrix. (a) Why is $A^{-1}$ also positive definite? (b) If $Q$ is orthogonal, why is $QAQ^T=QAQ^{-1}$ positive definite? State the test used. (c) Show that $B=\begin{pmatrix}A&A\\A&A\end{pmatrix}$ is positive semidefinite but not positive definite.",
 r"(a),(b) Both matrices are positive definite. (c) $B$ is positive semidefinite and singular.",
 r"(a) All eigenvalues of $A$ are positive. The eigenvalues of its inverse are their positive reciprocals. (b) For nonzero $x$, $x^TQAQ^Tx=(Q^Tx)^TA(Q^Tx)>0$ since $Q$ is invertible; this is the quadratic-form test. Alternatively, orthogonal similarity preserves the positive eigenvalues. (c) For any $x,y$, $(x^T,y^T)B(x^T,y^T)^T=(x+y)^TA(x+y)\ge0$. For $x=-y\ne0$ it equals zero, so $B$ is not positive definite.",[2],[2]),
(3,r"Let $A=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\4&0\end{pmatrix}$. (a) Find its eigenvalues and eigenvectors, and express $u(0)=(2,0)^T$ as their combination. (b) Solve $du/dt=Au$ with this initial value as a combination of eigenvectors. (c) Find an SVD $A=U\Sigma V^T$: first find $V,\Sigma$ from $A^TA$, then the orthonormal columns of $U$.",
 r"(a) $\lambda=\pm2i$, $v_+=(1,-2i)^T$, $v_-=(1,2i)^T$, $u(0)=v_++v_-$. (b) $u(t)=e^{2it}v_++e^{-2it}v_-=(2\cos2t,4\sin2t)^T$. (c) $V=I$, $\Sigma=\operatorname{diag}(4,1)$, $U=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$.",
 r"The characteristic polynomial is $\lambda^2+4$, giving the stated eigenpairs. The initial vector is their sum, so each eigenvector acquires the factor $e^{\lambda t}$ to solve the differential equation. For the SVD, $A^TA=\operatorname{diag}(16,1)$, so its normalized eigenvectors give $V=I$ and singular values 4,1. Then $u_j=Av_j/\sigma_j$, yielding $u_1=(0,1)^T$, $u_2=(-1,0)^T$. These columns are orthonormal and $U\Sigma V^T=A$.",[3],[3]),
]}

STATS1 = [
(2,r"Create a password as a string of 10 characters: 5 are letters, upper or lower case (52 choices), with repetition allowed; 3 are digits 0 through 9, with repetition allowed; 2 are distinct symbols from the five symbols !, @, #, dollar sign, and &. (a) How many passwords are possible? No need to simplify. (b) If all locations of symbols, letters, and numbers are equally likely, what is the probability that the two symbols are adjacent?",
 r"(a) $\binom{10}{2}\binom85\,20\,52^5\,10^3$. (b) $1/5$.",
 r"Choose the two symbol locations in $\binom{10}{2}$ ways, and their ordered distinct values in $5\cdot4$ ways. Choose the five letter locations from the remaining eight, and then choose letters and digits in $52^5$ and $10^3$ ways. For adjacency, there are 9 possible adjacent pairs of positions instead of $\binom{10}{2}=45$. All other factors cancel, giving $9/45=1/5$.",[1,2],[1,2]),
(3,r"You have five four-sided dice and three six-sided dice. Choose one uniformly at random from a cup, roll it, and report the result. Let $D$ be the number of sides of the chosen die and $R$ the roll. (a) Make the joint probability table, including both marginals. (b) Find $P(R=3)$. (c) Find $P(D=4\mid R=3)$. (d) Are $D$ and $R$ independent?",
 r"(a) For $r=1,2,3,4$, $P(R=r,D=4)=5/32$, $P(R=r,D=6)=1/16$, and $P(R=r)=7/32$. For $r=5,6$, these entries are $0,1/16,1/16$. The column marginals are $5/8,3/8$. (b) $7/32$. (c) $5/7$. (d) No.",
 r"The joint probabilities multiply the chance of choosing the die type by the conditional roll probability: $(5/8)(1/4)=5/32$ and $(3/8)(1/6)=1/16$. Adding rows and columns gives the marginals. Thus $P(R=3)=5/32+1/16=7/32$, and $P(D=4\mid R=3)=(5/32)/(7/32)=5/7$. Independence fails because $P(R=6,D=4)=0$ while $P(R=6)P(D=4)=5/128>0$.",[2],[2,3]),
(4,r"A disease screening test has outcomes positive, negative, and uncertain. For someone with the disease the respective probabilities are 70%, 10%, and 20%; without the disease they are 10%, 60%, and 30%. Disease prevalence is 2%. What is the probability that a randomly selected person who tests positive has the disease?",
 r"$1/8=0.125$.",
 r"Write $D$ for disease and $T$ for a positive test. Bayes' theorem gives $P(D\mid T)=\frac{P(T\mid D)P(D)}{P(T\mid D)P(D)+P(T\mid D^c)P(D^c)}=\frac{0.7\cdot0.02}{0.7\cdot0.02+0.1\cdot0.98}=1/8$.",[3],[3]),
(5,r"Xeno and Yolanda plan to meet at noon, but each will arrive between noon and 1pm. Let $X,Y$ be their respective lateness in hours, with joint density $f(x,y)=5/4-xy$ on $[0,1]^2$ and zero outside. (a) Find the marginal densities. (b) Are they independent? (c) Find $E[X]$ and $\operatorname{Var}(X)$, simplifying fractions. (d) Find covariance and correlation; symmetry gives the same mean and variance for $Y$. (e) Set up, but do not evaluate, the probability that Yolanda arrives after Xeno and within 6 minutes (0.1 hours) of him; specify both the integration region and integrand completely.",
 r"(a) $f_X(x)=5/4-x/2$ on $[0,1]$, $f_Y(y)=5/4-y/2$ on $[0,1]$, zero otherwise. (b) No. (c) $E[X]=11/24$, $\operatorname{Var}(X)=47/576$. (d) $\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=-5/576$, $\operatorname{Cor}(X,Y)=-5/47$. (e) $\iint_R(5/4-xy)\,dx\,dy$, $R=\{0\le x,y\le1,\ x<y<x+0.1\}$.",
 r"Integrating out the other variable gives the two marginals. Their product is not the joint density, so independence fails. Using $f_X$, $E[X]=\int_0^1 x(5/4-x/2)\,dx=11/24$ and $E[X^2]=7/24$. Thus $\operatorname{Var}(X)=7/24-(11/24)^2=47/576$. Also $E[XY]=\int_0^1\int_0^1xy(5/4-xy)\,dx\,dy=29/144$, so covariance is $29/144-(11/24)^2=-5/576$ and correlation is $(-5/576)/(47/576)=-5/47$. The requested region is the strip of the unit square between $y=x$ and $y=x+0.1$. An equivalent iterated expression is $\int_0^{0.9}\int_x^{x+0.1}(5/4-xy)\,dy\,dx+\int_{0.9}^1\int_x^1(5/4-xy)\,dy\,dx$.",[3,4],[3,4,5]),
(6,r"A solar panel company wants to maximize production in the next 20 days before a state subsidy expires. Daily outputs are independent with a gamma distribution whose density is $f(x)=\frac{x^4}{4!\,10^{10}}e^{-x/100}$ for $x>0$. The mean is 500 and variance $5\cdot10^4$. Estimate the probability of manufacturing more than 10,500 panels in the next 20 days.",
 r"Approximately $0.3085$ by the central limit theorem.",
 r"Let $S$ be total production. Independence gives $E[S]=20\cdot500=10000$ and $\operatorname{Var}(S)=20\cdot5\cdot10^4=10^6$, so $\sigma_S=1000$. The central limit theorem gives $P(S>10500)\approx P(Z>0.5)=1-0.6915=0.3085$.",[4],[5]),
]

STATS2 = [
('I_1',r"Determine whether each concept or statement is Frequentist, Bayesian, or both. Give a short explanation. (a) P-value. (b) Prior distribution. (c) Average of data. (d) 'There is a 3% probability that the average weight is between 92.3 mg and 100 mg.' (e) 'The odds in favor of $H_0$ against $H_A$ are 1 to 3.'",
 r"(a) Frequentist. (b) Bayesian. (c) Both. (d) Bayesian, interpreting average weight as the unknown population mean. (e) Bayesian.",
 r"P-values are used in frequentist null-hypothesis significance testing. Priors and odds between hypotheses assign probabilities to hypotheses and are Bayesian concepts. A sample average is a statistic used in either framework. In (d), interpreting the average weight as the unknown population mean, the probability statement is Bayesian because it assigns probability to a parameter interval.",[1],[1]),
('I_2',r"A significance test uses significance level $0.025$ and has power 95%. Give an answer and short explanation for each part. (a) Assuming the null hypothesis, what is the probability of a type I error? (b) Assuming the alternative hypothesis, what is the probability of a type II error? For each part, choose from (i) 0.025, (ii) 0.05, (iii) 0.95, (iv) 0.975, (v) cannot be determined from the information given.",
 r"(a) (i), $0.025$. (b) (ii), $0.05$.",
 r"By definition, the type I error probability is $P(\text{reject }H_0\mid H_0)=\alpha=0.025$. The type II error probability is $P(\text{do not reject }H_0\mid H_A)=1-\text{power}=1-0.95=0.05$.",[1],[1]),
('I_4',r"You find a coin with unknown probability $\theta$ of landing heads. Select the only reasonable prior for $\theta$ from the given choices. No explanation is needed.",
 'B',r"$\operatorname{Beta}(2,2)$ has support $[0,1]$ and is symmetric between heads and tails. The uniform prior restricted to $[0,0.5]$ excludes $\theta>0.5$. The normal prior assigns positive probability outside $[0,1]$.",[2],[2]),
('I_5',r"For each case, determine whether the prior is conjugate to the likelihood. Parameters $a,b$ belong to the priors and $c_1$ is a normalizing constant. (a) $\theta\in[0,1]$, prior $c_1\theta^a(1-\theta)^b$, likelihood $\binom{10}{x}\theta^x(1-\theta)^{10-x}$. (b) $\lambda\in[0,\infty)$, prior $c_1\lambda^{a-1}e^{-b\lambda}$, likelihood $\lambda e^{-\lambda x}$.",
 r"Yes in both cases.",
 r"In (a), prior times likelihood is proportional to $\theta^{a+x}(1-\theta)^{b+10-x}$, of the same beta form. In (b), it is proportional to $\lambda^ae^{-(b+x)\lambda}$, of the same gamma form. Both posterior families therefore agree with their prior families.",[2],[2]),
('II_1',r"A gamma distribution with shape 3 and unknown rate $\beta>0$ has density $f(x)=\beta^3x^2e^{-\beta x}/2$ on $(0,\infty)$. The data $1,1,2,3,5$ were drawn independently from it. Find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\beta$.",
 r"$\hat\beta=5/4$.",
 r"The likelihood is $\beta^{15}\prod_{i=1}^5x_i^2\,e^{-\beta\sum x_i}/2^5$. Thus the log likelihood is $\ell(\beta)=15\ln\beta-\beta\sum x_i+C$. Its derivative is $15/\beta-12$, giving $\hat\beta=15/12=5/4$. The second derivative is $-15/\beta^2<0$.",[2],[3]),
('II_2',r"A random process has outcomes $A,B,C$ with probabilities $\theta/2,\theta/2,1-\theta$, where $0\le\theta\le1$ is unknown. Its prior density is $f(\theta)=3\theta^2$. Five independent runs produce $A,B,C,A,B$. Find the posterior density of $\theta$.",
 r"$f(\theta\mid\text{data})=56\theta^6(1-\theta)$ on $[0,1]$, zero outside; a $\operatorname{Beta}(7,2)$ density.",
 r"The likelihood of the ordered data is $(\theta/2)^4(1-\theta)$. Multiplying by the prior gives the unnormalized density $(3/16)\theta^6(1-\theta)$. Its integral is $(3/16)(1/7-1/8)=(3/16)/56$, so normalization gives $56\theta^6(1-\theta)$.",[3],[3]),
('II_3',r"In the year 2122 a screening test for seven-year-olds detects Bayesian intelligence, an early superpower that lets children answer statistics questions correctly. Prior odds that a randomly selected child has superpowers are $1/100$. The test has a 100% true positive rate and a 10% false positive rate. If a randomly selected child answers correctly, what are the posterior odds that the child has superpowers?",
 r"$1/10$, or $1:10$ in favor of superpowers.",
 r"Posterior odds equal prior odds times the likelihood ratio. The likelihood ratio for a correct answer is $1/(1/10)=10$. Thus the posterior odds are $(1/100)\cdot10=1/10$.",[3,4],[3,4]),
('II_4',r"You perform a right-tailed one-sided $Z$-test at significance level 0.1 and observe $Z=2$. (a) Which R expression below computes the critical value? (b) Compute that critical value from standard normal tables. (c) Which expression computes the p-value? (d) Compute that p-value from tables. (e) Should you reject the null? The expressions for (a) and (c) are: (i) pnorm(0.1,0,1), (ii) pnorm(0.9,0,1), (iii) pnorm(0.95,0,1), (iv) pnorm(2.0,0,1), (v) 1-pnorm(2.0,0,1), (vi) qnorm(0.05,0,1), (vii) qnorm(0.1,0,1), (viii) qnorm(0.9,0,1), (ix) qnorm(0.95,0,1).",
 r"(a) (viii), qnorm(0.9,0,1). (b) Approximately $1.28$. (c) (v), 1-pnorm(2.0,0,1). (d) Approximately $0.0228$. (e) Reject $H_0$.",
 r"The right-tail rejection region must have probability 0.1, so its boundary has cumulative probability 0.9 and is $\Phi^{-1}(0.9)\approx1.28$. The observed right-tail probability is $1-\Phi(2)\approx1-0.9772=0.0228$. This is below 0.1, or equivalently $2>1.28$, so reject the null hypothesis.",[4],[4,5]),
('II_5',r"A study compares allele frequencies in diabetics and nondiabetics. For genotypes Bb or bb, the counts are 15 diabetic and 5 normal; for BB, they are 35 diabetic and 45 normal. Test whether the genotype-category frequencies differ between the two groups at significance level 0.05.",
 r"$\chi^2=6.25$ with one degree of freedom; reject equal frequencies at level 0.05 ($p\approx0.0124$).",
 r"Use a chi-square homogeneity test. Under $H_0$ the category frequencies agree between groups; under $H_A$ they differ. Each group has 50 subjects and the pooled Bb/bb fraction is $20/100=0.2$, so expected counts are 10,10,40,40. The statistic is $25/10+25/10+25/40+25/40=6.25$. The degrees of freedom are $(2-1)(2-1)=1$. The source table gives $0.01<p<0.025$, and the 5% critical value is about 3.84. Thus reject $H_0$.",[5],[5,6]),
]
